    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d93">

                <pb facs="0131" n="121" sameAs="frus:frus1948v07/pg121"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/2–2848: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Mukden</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Ward</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mukden</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-02-28T19:00:00+08:00">February 28,
                            1948—7 p. m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 1—9:04 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>106. Yingkow captured by Communists February 26. Speed with which capture
                    executed indicates Government forces, consisting 1 division plus 2 regiments
                    former PPC<anchor n="80" xml:id="d93-f1"/> troops, poorly equipped and trained
                    and low morale caused by Government failure furnish equipment <pb facs="0132"
                        n="122" xml:id="pg122"/> and ammunition although having knowledge shortage
                    since early summer, put up very little or no resistance. Communist radio
                    reports, probably accurate, state Wang Chia-shan, C. O. Government’s 50th [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">58th</hi>] Division of Fifty-second Army, seized
                    complete power Ying-kow and surrendered city together with 10,000 troops to
                    Communists.</p>
                <p>Government leaders including Mayor Yuan Hung-ta and Cheng Ming-hsin, Chief Staff
                    who resisted surrender, were taken prisoner by Wang and troops desiring resist
                    Communists were disarmed.</p>
                <p>Sent Embassy, repeated Department 106, Tientsin 65, Peiping 75.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ward</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d93-f1" n="80" xml:id="d93-f1-content">
                <p>Peace Preservation Corps.</p>
            </note>
